1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:05,702
Here are three clouds of points, and no labels at all.

2
00:00:05,982 --> 00:00:10,182
I drag the slider back to the start, and now watch the black crosses.

3
00:00:10,463 --> 00:00:17,062
Each step, every point joins its nearest cross, and then every cross moves to the middle of its points.

4
00:00:17,343 --> 00:00:24,183
Step by step, the crosses settle, and after nine steps nothing moves any more: that's K-means.

5
00:00:24,463 --> 00:00:28,207
But the second half of this video is about the places where it breaks.

6
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:32,831
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

7
00:00:33,631 --> 00:00:37,471
In supervised learning you give the model examples with answers.

8
00:00:37,751 --> 00:00:43,727
In unsupervised learning there are no answers, and the algorithm has to find structure on its own.

9
00:00:44,007 --> 00:00:46,959
Clustering means finding groups of similar points.

10
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:49,423
K-means is the classic way to do it.

11
00:00:49,703 --> 00:00:55,007
You choose how many groups you want, called k, and the algorithm finds k centres.

12
00:00:55,287 --> 00:00:59,343
It's fast, it's simple, and it's usually the first thing people try.

13
00:00:59,623 --> 00:01:04,975
But it carries a hidden assumption about the shape of the groups, and that's what the second tab tests.

14
00:01:05,775 --> 00:01:08,151
The whole algorithm is a short loop..

15
00:01:08,431 --> 00:01:12,366
First, pick k of the points at random as the starting centres.

16
00:01:12,646 --> 00:01:17,446
That seed is a setting in the app, because a different start can give a different answer.

17
00:01:17,726 --> 00:01:19,598
Then loop.

18
00:01:19,878 --> 00:01:26,791
The first line computes the distance from every point to every centre and takes the nearest: that's the assignment step.

19
00:01:27,071 --> 00:01:32,687
The next line moves each centre to the mean of the points assigned to it, which is the update step.

20
00:01:32,967 --> 00:01:36,087
If no centre moved, we've converged and stop.

21
00:01:36,367 --> 00:01:40,159
I keep every step in a list so the slider can scrub through them.

22
00:01:40,959 --> 00:01:43,983
This is the Blobs dataset with three clusters.

23
00:01:44,263 --> 00:01:47,431
At the start the three crosses are three random points.

24
00:01:47,711 --> 00:01:54,215
The number on the right is the within-cluster distance: the total squared distance from each point to its own centre.

25
00:01:54,495 --> 00:01:57,423
At iteration zero it is four hundred and fifty.

26
00:01:57,703 --> 00:02:00,127
K-means is trying to make that number small.

27
00:02:00,407 --> 00:02:02,879
I step forward one iteration at a time.

28
00:02:03,159 --> 00:02:13,983
The crosses jump towards the middle of their groups, and the distance falls: two hundred and twenty-four, then one hundred and eighty-three, one hundred and seventy-one, then one sixty-eight.

29
00:02:14,263 --> 00:02:16,519
The improvements get smaller each time.

30
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:23,567
The distance stops changing at about the fifth step, and the loop stops after nine, with the app marking it converged.

31
00:02:23,847 --> 00:02:26,871
The final distance is one hundred and sixty-seven.

32
00:02:27,151 --> 00:02:33,751
K-means never makes it worse, but it only finds a local optimum, so a different seed can end somewhere different.

33
00:02:34,551 --> 00:02:37,239
There's one more thing: how do you choose k?

34
00:02:37,519 --> 00:02:43,687
The app has an elbow chart that runs K-means for k from one to nine and plots the final distance.

35
00:02:43,967 --> 00:02:50,663
It falls from six hundred at k equals one to three hundred and fourteen at two and to one sixty-seven at three.

36
00:02:50,943 --> 00:02:57,039
After three, it keeps falling, but slowly, so the bend, the elbow, is at three.

37
00:02:57,319 --> 00:02:59,671
That matches how this data was generated.

38
00:03:00,471 --> 00:03:05,007
The second tab runs four algorithms on five datasets, all at once.

39
00:03:05,287 --> 00:03:16,639
K-means, a Gaussian mixture, which fits ellipses, agglomerative clustering with single linkage, which merges close neighbours, and DBSCAN, which grows clusters from dense regions..

40
00:03:16,919 --> 00:03:20,495
Each row is a dataset, each column an algorithm.

41
00:03:21,295 --> 00:03:23,167
Look at the Moons row.

42
00:03:23,447 --> 00:03:25,439
Two interleaved half circles.

43
00:03:25,719 --> 00:03:32,103
K-means cuts straight through the middle instead, because it only knows how to draw round groups around a centre.

44
00:03:32,383 --> 00:03:35,767
The agglomerative and DBSCAN columns follow the curves.

45
00:03:36,047 --> 00:03:41,447
I also computed a score offline, the adjusted Rand index, where one is perfect.

46
00:03:41,727 --> 00:03:50,079
For the moons, K-means scores zero point four six, while single linkage gets one point zero and DBSCAN zero point nine nine.

47
00:03:50,359 --> 00:03:59,311
The circles are worse for K-means: a ring around a smaller ring has no useful straight cut, and its score is zero, which is no better than chance.

48
00:03:59,591 --> 00:04:09,911
DBSCAN does well, though its score is zero point eight six, because with this setting it splits the data into three clusters, and the grey points are the ones it calls noise.

49
00:04:10,191 --> 00:04:18,830
On the stretched blobs, which are tilted ellipses, K-means only knows round shapes, while the Gaussian mixture can model tilted ones.

50
00:04:19,111 --> 00:04:25,759
On the uniform noise row there are no real groups at all, and most of the algorithms still carve it into pieces.

51
00:04:26,039 --> 00:04:30,911
Clustering will happily return an answer, whether or not there is anything to find.

52
00:04:31,710 --> 00:04:39,030
Every algorithm hides an assumption about what a cluster looks like: round, elliptical, dense, or connected.

53
00:04:39,311 --> 00:04:44,495
When your data matches it you win, and when it doesn't, you get a confident wrong answer.

54
00:04:44,775 --> 00:04:46,647
So plot your data first.

55
00:04:47,446 --> 00:04:53,374
This is two-dimensional toy data, which is much friendlier than real data with dozens of columns.

56
00:04:53,655 --> 00:05:01,095
I used default-ish settings: a fixed k of two on the comparison tab, one DBSCAN radius, and single linkage only.

57
00:05:01,375 --> 00:05:08,839
The scores I quoted were computed offline with the same generators and are not shown in the app, and I only ran one random seed.

58
00:05:09,119 --> 00:05:14,471
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but you should read it yourself too.

59
00:05:15,271 --> 00:05:24,271
Next I'd let you upload your own CSV, add a silhouette score so you can judge clusters without true labels, and let you tune each algorithm's settings.

60
00:05:24,551 --> 00:05:29,039
The code is in the description, with no API key and no cost.

61
00:05:29,318 --> 00:05:37,047
If you liked watching K-means think, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which dataset should break an algorithm next.
